#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176655} :activity/id #uuid "275b0afa-011e-599e-83db-08f12becae8b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049126 :topic/id #uuid "dcb0f682-2f5d-57d5-ac9a-10eb5e8a8b36" :topic/math-academy-id 3591} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176950 :tutorial/content "Given two finite data sets of equal length, say,\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\},\n$$\n\nthe **covariance** between $x$ and $y,$ denoted by $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ is a measure that evaluates the extent to which $x$ and $y$ change together.\n\n- A positive covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is also likely to increase:\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A negative covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is likely to decrease:\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\n- A covariance of zero (or close to zero) indicates that there is no *linear* relationship between $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/a5/a58fa4d40d77af23df86b5bd8eaca3119b93bc9ea97cc435eb4e53ba2777b071.png)\n\n \n\n**Watch Out!** A covariance of zero does *not* mean that $x$ and $y$ are not related. It simply means the relationship between them is not linear. To illustrate, consider the image below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4bdbf92e691174644dda9b9a84acb1b896c0436afb221501dc10b4635841539.png)\n\n \n\nThe (nonlinear) relationship shown above is $y=x^2.$ However, it can be shown that $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = 0$ for this data set.\n\nTo calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ we use the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$  and $\\overline{y}$ denote the means of $x$ and $y$ respectively.\n\n**Note**: This formula for the covariance assumes that $x$ and $y$ are drawn from a *population*. There is a slightly different formula for covariance when $x$ and $y$ are sample data. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." :tutorial/id #uuid "da03b3e7-b452-59b9-b81e-5dd75cea02a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10301 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a9620c91-7f1f-5c90-81f9-15b63580b9b6" :step/math-academy-id 217080 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175746 :question/id #uuid "ef520918-4e4b-516b-acaa-57a14b5c1405" :question/math-academy-id "e-16134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050862}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566235}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "7c94f745-4162-5e7d-841a-0a7d3f00e84a" :step/math-academy-id 217081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175747 :question/id #uuid "33adfe2b-297e-5c52-8c4f-088d0c2b66e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535993}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance in Context"} :step/id #uuid "48e20fe8-234e-5200-b8a8-908f518d0da4" :step/math-academy-id 217084 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176951 :tutorial/content "Variance measures the spread of a *single* data set $x$ around its mean value $\\overline{x}.$ On the other hand, covariance measures the relationship between *two* data sets $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe (population) covariance of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad  \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nIf we compare a single data set, say $x$, against itself using our formula for covariance, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,x) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2$ denotes the variance of $x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "42b9ca98-be0b-50e0-9b90-f3e38732fcd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10305 :tutorial/title "The Connection Between Variance and Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "2661268b-e076-5805-af10-02ffd1c3fc5e" :step/math-academy-id 217087}] :activity/title "Covariance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176135}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566300} :activity/id #uuid "b71da19e-d859-5f56-b0d6-c095ea38a156" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049126 :topic/id #uuid "dcb0f682-2f5d-57d5-ac9a-10eb5e8a8b36" :topic/math-academy-id 3591} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88b47b3a-e1eb-56e3-8e5d-f4a75117a124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267359} {:answer/id #uuid "939eb21e-65d9-50c2-9f13-2a9c5beb9a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267466} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ce4537-3c51-5a6d-afe7-2fec5be72a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268011} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e26f0a-1b74-5e94-b5a5-bc4046928faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268228} {:answer/id #uuid "fec05032-593e-5492-9092-712f5a38fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267466} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c10a926-37e1-5762-a3bc-8007b371df78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a871439-561a-57b5-a2c7-bd992bf74497" :question/math-academy-id "q-134173" :question/problem "| **Time Spent Practicing, \nin hours ($x$)** | $4$ | $5$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Points Scored ($y$)** | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ | $16$ |\n\nThe table above shows Mary's time practicing her jump shot each week and the number of points she scores in the Friday varsity basketball game. Compute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=10.$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-6)(4-10)+(5-6)(8-10)+(7-6)(12-10)+(8-6)(16-10)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-2)(-6)+(-1)(-2)+(1)(2)+(2)(6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[12+2+2+12] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6dca6571-a5ec-5352-8411-48e22666e7db"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20dc8ecd-d3e7-5b4f-99fd-e340125220ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266439} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf4b541-acde-57aa-9576-c308680b6c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266669} {:answer/id #uuid "756ab281-4dfc-522d-827d-e6219862d3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267179} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdc279d-d8bf-5db2-99d2-0230056971c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267242} {:answer/id #uuid "9717bf9a-a903-59dd-8538-a467393f5b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267242} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb084b7-92a4-50b3-ab5e-874dc5122653" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3d02030-a1e3-5cc4-b308-6cf0c2ca9175" :question/math-academy-id "q-134168" :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{-4,\\,2,\\,6,\\,8\\},\\,y=\\{2,\\,-9,\\,3,\\,12\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-4)+2+6+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (2+(-9)+3+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-4-3)(2-2)+(2-3)(-9-2)+(6-3)(3-2)+(8-3)(12-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-7)(0)+(-1)(-11)+(3)(1)+(5)(10)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[0+11+3+50] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9360c9ad-0d01-58c3-bea9-ccb29100787e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c9e72d7-60a7-512f-af6c-41536483d13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565876} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8c39eb-cc31-5a79-a9bd-500afe12c950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565878} {:answer/id #uuid "dc079189-f549-5385-8ccd-d06d3585f132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566035} {:answer/id #uuid "f0431254-6c69-543d-b05b-52b952950087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566075} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2cb0ca-1fb3-54af-9579-b241fc473f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566035} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3bb762e-f253-556e-b239-591405629ea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64eb2650-1408-5539-acdf-62ad07765696" :question/math-academy-id "q-134161" :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{4,\\,4,\\,-4,\\,4\\},\\,y=\\{-1,\\,-2,\\,7,\\,4\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+4+(-4)+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-1)+(-2)+7+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-2)(-1-2)+(4-2)(-2-2)+(-4-2)(7-2)+(4-2)(4-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2)(-3)+(2)(-4)+(-6)(5)+(2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-6-8-30+4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-40) \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "96cf8720-bbba-5c5c-9ffc-98b6e94d3c0b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566299}}] :activity/title "Covariance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565672}]}
